  "origin": "Roughly 6 million US federal and state court decisions (~40 million pages) digitized from hardbound court reporters by the Harvard Library Innovation Lab in collaboration with Ravel Law. Original judicial opinions are in the public domain (no copyright subsists in US judicial work product); the digitized full text was under access limitations under the original Harvard-Ravel collaboration agreement until March 2024, when those limitations expired and the data was fully released without restriction on access or use. Aggregated by Pleias into the OpenGovernment collection (Legal Commons) of Common Corpus. Form: digitized + OCR'd, then aggregated. Note: the license field uses the CC Public Domain Mark IRI per the interim convention in spec/0.0.1/index.md; the underlying works are public domain by US judicial-work-product doctrine, which is a jurisdiction-scoped status rather than a single globally-applicable license term. The `licenseScope` field (added to 0.0.1 after this stress test surfaced Gap A) carries 'US' to qualify the PD claim to the US jurisdiction — see notes/stress-test-pleias-common-corpus.md (Gap A).",
